/// Synopsis: a light color (a luma of 70% or more: `secondary` and `secondary-light`, the `-lighter` and `-lightest` tones, `quaternary-light`) in a box, a callout, a question and an example. The badge of the box has dark blue text, and what takes the color of the box (bold, alert, highlight, chip, link, icon, markers, arrows, the numbers of the lists of a question or an example, the label and the icon of a bar callout, the text and the logo of the footer of a question) is in the dark tone of its family, `secondary-dark` for the yellow, or, out of the palette, in the color darkened by 40%. The `===` pill of a yellow box is dark yellow with white text, and an alert in it is dark blue, as the yellow does not read on the dark yellow. The border of a question and the rules of its footer, its bottom border, keep the color of the box. In a box or a callout the numbers of the lists stay blue (page 3). Pages 5 to 7: a yellow question with a `===` repeated after `---` (pages 5 and 6) and the answer in the dark tone (the dashed rule, and the word a little darker), and a light green example with its solution. Page 8: `#secondary`, `#tertiary` and `#quaternary` in light pills, tags and a light cell keep their color where it reads (3:1 or more: the red on the yellow) and otherwise take the color of an alert there: blue in a light pill or tag (a yellow word in a yellow badge, a green word in a light green tag), the dark yellow in a light yellow cell; in the dark yellow `===` pill the green word turns dark blue, as on any dark pill.
#import "../../common.typ": *
#show: setup
#include "body.typ"
